# Project Log: Building & Defending a Public Web Application

This is the fuller story behind the [README](./README.md) — the actual build-test-break-fix journey, including the parts that didn't work the first time. Live infrastructure details (real domain, IPs, credentials) are intentionally left out; everything here is about the tools, the failures, and what they taught.

---

## 1. Starting Local, Then Going Public

The first attempt at this project was a local VirtualBox VM. It crashed mid-write when its disk filled up unexpectedly — a database process couldn't save and the whole VM had to be force-closed and scrapped.

**Lesson:** plan disk size upfront, and don't let a VM's disk fill silently. It's a boring failure mode, but it wastes real time if you don't catch it early.

That crash was also a useful forcing function: the project needed to be publicly accessible and sit behind a real CDN/WAF, which meant a local VM was the wrong tool from the start. Shared hosting was ruled out too — no root access, no ability to install Docker or run security tooling. A real VPS with full root access was the only option that actually fit the requirements.

---

## 2. The Foundation

SSH access was locked down early — key-based authentication only, password login disabled outright. A basic firewall was configured to only allow the minimum necessary ports, and automated ban tooling was added to handle repeated failed login attempts.

The application itself was built as three separate containers, orchestrated together: a reverse proxy in front, a Python backend handling logic and authentication, and a database behind it, isolated from any direct internet access. Splitting these into containers meant each piece could be restarted, rebuilt, or patched independently without taking down the others.

One early habit that paid off repeatedly: taking a system snapshot after every stage that reached a stable, working state — *before* touching anything new. When later stages went sideways (and they did, more than once), having a known-good checkpoint mattered more than any real-time debugging skill.

---

## 3. The SIEM Install That Wouldn't Cooperate

Installing the SIEM/EDR platform was, by a wide margin, the hardest part of this project. Not because any single step was conceptually difficult — because of how many ways a multi-component install could quietly break itself.

**Round 1:** The manager, indexer, and dashboard all installed cleanly together and worked. Then, thinking a local agent was needed to monitor the host itself, a separate agent package was installed alongside the manager on the same machine.

That silently removed the manager. Manager and agent packages conflict on a single box and can't coexist — the dashboard just started throwing errors, with no obvious link back to the agent install.

**Round 2:** Reinstalling the manager pulled a different version than what the indexer and dashboard were running. Mismatched versions across components caused ongoing instability until everything was pinned to the exact same release.

**Round 3:** After the version mismatch, the dashboard started returning authentication errors instead of the login page. The internal service account credentials the dashboard used no longer matched what the indexer expected — a side effect of `apt remove --purge` *not* actually removing all the data directories from previous install attempts. Stale security config data was surviving reinstalls and clashing with fresh ones.

**The Timeshift detour:** rather than keep chasing the credential mismatch, the plan was to roll back to a clean pre-install snapshot using the system's backup/restore tool. The restore process segfaulted immediately after the bootloader-reinstall step — twice, with different settings. Digging into this later confirmed it's a known, long-standing, unresolved bug in that tool specifically on virtualized/cloud environments — the tool assumes a physical machine's boot process, which cloud VPS environments don't replicate correctly.

**Lesson:** snapshot *creation* isn't the same as reliable *restore*. Always test the restore path before trusting it as a safety net — and if a tool wasn't designed with your environment in mind (cloud vs. physical hardware), don't assume it'll behave the same way.

**The actual fix:** manual cleanup. Every stale directory left behind by the failed installs and incomplete purges had to be found and force-deleted by hand, then the SIEM components reinstalled one at a time (not via the all-in-one install flag) to isolate any remaining issues. That clean, step-by-step install finally completed without errors.

Along the way, two more small but instructive problems came up: the install script rejected the server's real public IP during multi-node config generation (it assumes private IPs for clustered setups — using localhost addressing was the correct fix for a single-box install), and the dashboard's default port collided with the web application's own HTTPS port, requiring the dashboard to be moved to a dedicated port.

**Bigger lesson from this whole saga:** package removal tools often don't fully clean up application data directories. When reinstalling something that's misbehaving, verify — don't assume — that a "removed" component is actually gone.

