## 4. Turning On Real Network Visibility

With the SIEM stable, a network intrusion detection tool was added to actually watch traffic at the packet level, not just application logs. The default configuration assumed a private, internal network — which is wrong for a public-facing server — so the tool's understanding of "which traffic is mine vs. external" had to be corrected before it worked meaningfully.

Wiring its alerts into the SIEM was a matter of pointing the SIEM's log collector at the new tool's output file. Once connected, alert volume in the dashboard jumped noticeably — file integrity monitoring events were now joined by real network-level detections.

**Real-world validation:** not long after, a genuine spike in alerts appeared — dozens of password-guessing attempts against SSH, all flagged under recognizable attack categories. Checking the actual source IP on those events (rather than assuming it was noise from the project's own testing) confirmed it: a real external attacker, not internal activity. The detection stack caught it, logged it, and the automated ban tooling was actively working against a completely different attacking IP in parallel.

Because SSH was already locked to key-only authentication, the brute-force attempts were guaranteed to fail regardless of how many were tried — but seeing the full detection chain fire correctly against a real, unprompted attack was worth more than any synthetic test could have been.

**Lesson:** a spike in alerts isn't automatically bad news, and it isn't automatically an attack either — always check the specific events behind a spike before reacting. More than once during this project, a scary-looking alert spike turned out to just be normal reconnect traffic from active development work.

