## 7. Chasing a Ghost: The Connectivity Investigation

Partway through the project, SSH and basic network reachability to the server started failing — but only from one specific network. Systematically ruling out causes mattered more than guessing:

- Checked server health directly through the hosting provider's own control panel and browser-based terminal — CPU, memory, and uptime all looked completely normal, ruling out a crash or resource exhaustion.
- Checked active connection counts and system load — nothing resembling a denial-of-service pattern.
- Tested from a second, different network expecting it to work as a "control" — it also failed, until it became clear that network blocked basic reachability testing *universally*, for everyone, unrelated to this server at all. A control test is only useful if you've verified it's actually a valid control.
- Ruled out the CDN as a cause entirely, since SSH traffic never routes through it in the first place — only the domain's web traffic does.

The most likely remaining explanation was carrier-side throttling or filtering building up gradually on that one specific network — not something fixable from the server side. A reliable fallback (the hosting provider's own browser-based terminal, which routes around the affected network path entirely) was adopted for whenever that specific network was in use.

**Lesson:** when isolating a network problem, always verify your "control" test doesn't have its own unrelated restrictions — otherwise a control test can produce a false confirmation instead of real signal.

---

## 8. What This Project Actually Taught

- **Defaults are not accidents.** Security features that ship "off" by default (cookie flags, restrictive service accounts) usually exist because the tool's authors can't predict every legitimate use case a stricter default might break — but that means it's on you to turn them on deliberately.
- **Layered defense means no single failure is fatal.** More than one control in this project turned out to have a real gap at some point — but because multiple independent layers existed, no single gap ever became a full compromise.
- **Tooling can lie to you in specific, structural ways** — a restore feature that doesn't match its creation feature's reliability, a firewall rule that looks correct but never actually gets evaluated, a test run from the wrong vantage point producing misleading results. Understanding *why* a tool failed matters more than just finding a workaround.
- **Knowing when to stop is a skill.** More than once, the right call was reverting a change and living with a known, lower-priority gap rather than pushing further into an unfamiliar, high-risk fix under time pressure.

